window.docContents[88].resolve({"/Solver/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-API-for-CVC5--Solver--Check-sat-and-modal-environments":{"contents":"The check-sat function is significantly more complex, to see why let us look a the get-value function first.\n\nThe value the model gives a term, as the Lean value its index describes.\n\nSince retrieving the value of a term is only legal (directly) after a check-sat that returned sat, cvc.lean strongly-types this fact with a modal environment, i.e. a monad specific to a solver being in sat-mode.\n\nSat-specific modal environment.\n\nCvc.lean has three of these, the other two being unknown-modeUsually triggered by a timeout or when in an undecidable theory. and unsat-mode.\n\nGet the unsatisfiable core.SMT-LIB:\\verbatim embed:rst:leading-asterisk\n.. code:: smtlib(get-unsat-core)Requires to enable option produce-unsat-cores.Note: In contrast to SMT-LIB, cvc5's API does not distinguish between named and\nunnamed assertions when producing an unsatisfiable core. Additionally, the API\nallows this option to be called after a check with assumptions. A subset of\nthose assumptions may be included in the unsatisfiable core returned by this\nfunction.Returns a set of terms representing the unsatisfiable core.\n\nUnsat-specific modal environment.\n\nGet a timeout core.This function computes a subset of the current assertions that couse a timeout. It may make multiple\nchecks for satisfiability internally, each limited by the timeout value given by\ntimeout-core-timeout.If the result is unknown and the reason is timeout, then returned the set of assertions corresponds\nto a subset of the current assertions that cause a timeout in the specified time\ntimeout-core-timeout. If the result is unsat, then the list of formulas correspond to an unsat\ncore for the current assertions. Otherwise, the result is sat, indicating that the current\nassertions are satisfiable, and the returned set of assertions is empty.(get-timeout-core)\nWarning: this function is experimental and may change in future versions.\n\nUnknown-specific modal environment.\n\nTo guarantee modal code is safe to run, cvc.lean does not allow users to directly run it: it must be passed alongside a check-sat query to guarantee that only the appropriate sat/unsat/unknown code runs. Hence the signature of the solver check-sat function:\n\nChecks the satisfiability of the constraints asserted so far.\n\nThe only argument left to explain is assuming which is an optional array of Term Bool providing additional assumptions for this check-sat, called check-sat-assuming in SMT-LIB 2.7.\n\n#eval Env.runIO do\n  let solver ← Solver.new\n  solver.setOpt .smt_produceModels true\n  solver.setOpt .smt_produceUnsatCores true\n  let b ← Term.mkSymbol \"b\"\n  let i1 ← Term.mkSymbol \"i1\"\n  let i2 ← Term.mkSymbol \"i2\"\n  let not_b ← smt! ¬ b\n  smt! i2 = 7 >>= solver.assert\n  smt! 0 ≤ i1 >>= solver.assert\n  smt! b ∨ i1 < 0 >>= solver.assert\n\n  let core ← solver.checkSat (assuming := #[not_b])\n    (ifUnsat := solver.getUnsatCore)\n  println! \"unsat core, not necessarily minimal:\"\n  for t in core do println! \"- {t}\"\n\n  -- `Solver.getValue` extracts lean values\n  let (bVal, i1Val, i2Val) ← solver.checkSat\n    (ifSat := return (\n      ← solver.getValue b,\n      -- there's also a `getValue` function on terms\n      ← i1.getValue,\n      ← solver.getValue i2))\n  println! \"model as Lean values:\\n\\\n    - b  := {bVal}\\n- i1 := {i1Val}\\n- i2 := {i2Val}\"\n\n  -- `Solver.getValueTerm` extracts (typed) cvc terms\n  let (bVal, i1Val, i2Val) ← solver.checkSat\n    (ifSat := return (\n      ← solver.getValueTerm b,\n      -- there's also a `getValueTerm` function on terms\n      ← i1.getValueTerm,\n      ← solver.getValueTerm i2))\n  println! \"model as cvc terms:\\n\\\n    - b  := {bVal}\\n- i1 := {i1Val}\\n- i2 := {i2Val}\"\n\n  -- a typed term can always be relaxed if needed\n  let vals ← solver.checkSat\n    (ifSat := do\n      let bVal ← solver.getValueTerm b\n      let i1Val ← solver.getValueTerm i1\n      let i2Val ← solver.getValueTerm i2\n      return #[ bVal.relax, i1Val.relax, i2Val.relax ])\n  println! \"untyped model: {vals}\"\n\n\nunsat core, not necessarily minimal:\n- (not b)\n- (= i2 7)\n- (<= 0 i1)\n- (or b (< i1 0))\nmodel as Lean values:\n- b  := true\n- i1 := 0\n- i2 := 7\nmodel as cvc terms:\n- b  := true\n- i1 := 0\n- i2 := 7\nuntyped model: #[true, 0, 7]\n\n\n","context":"Cvc.lean -- High-Level API for CVC5\u0009Solver","header":"6.2. Check-sat and modal environments","id":"/Solver/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-API-for-CVC5--Solver--Check-sat-and-modal-environments"}});